\section{Driving force versus decay: the escape race}
\label{sec:theory}

Under \cref{ass:aligned} with $\Pred_0=p_0I$, \cref{sec:exact} gives the exact structure, known in substance, and \cref{sec:race} the race criterion, an approximation whose accuracy \cref{sec:experiments} measures; proofs are in \cref{app:proofs}.

\subsection{Exact structure}
\label{sec:exact}

\begin{proposition}[Exact mode reduction; per-mode system of \citealp{tian2021understanding}]
\label{prop:reduction}
Let \cref{ass:aligned}(i) hold, $d\ge n$, $\Pred_0=p_0I$, $\Wc_0=\sum_k a_k(0)\,\tilde u_ku_k^\top$ with orthonormal $\tilde u_k\in\R^d$, and $\Wt_0=\Wc_0$.
Then for all $t$, $\Wc=\sum_ka_k\tilde u_ku_k^\top$, $\Wt=\sum_k\bar a_k\tilde u_ku_k^\top$ and $\Pred=\sum_kp_k\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top+p_\perp\big(I-\sum_k\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top\big)$, where
\begin{equation}
\dot a_k=p_kc_k\bar a_k-\big(p_k^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W\big)a_k,\qquad
\dot p_k=\alpha\big[(c_k\bar a_k-p_k\sigma_ka_k)\,a_k-\lambda_Pp_k\big],\qquad
\dot{\bar a}_k=\kappa(a_k-\bar a_k),
\label{eq:reduced}
\end{equation}
and $\dot p_\perp=-\alpha\lambda_Pp_\perp$.
With the copy target ($\bar a_k=a_k$),
\begin{equation}
\dot a_k=a_k\,g_k(p_k),\qquad
g_k(p)=\underbrace{p\,c_k}_{\text{drive}}-\underbrace{\big(p^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W\big)}_{\text{decay}},\qquad
\dot p_k=\alpha\big[(c_k-p_k\sigma_k)\,a_k^2-\lambda_Pp_k\big].
\label{eq:growth}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
At $\alpha=1$ this is Tian et al.'s per-mode system (their eqs.~11--12), here with anisotropic $(c_k,\sigma_k)$ and an EMA target.
The rank is the number of surviving amplitudes $a_k$, and a direction grows only while its gain lies in the \emph{window} where $g_k(p)>0$,
\begin{equation}
p\in\big(p_-^k,p_+^k\big),\qquad p_\pm^k=\big(c_k\pm\sqrt{c_k^2-4\lambda_W\sigma_k}\big)/(2\sigma_k),
\label{eq:window}
\end{equation}
non-empty if and only if $c_k^2>4\lambda_W\sigma_k$.

\begin{proposition}[Fixed points and their stability]
\label{prop:fixed}
Take the copy target and $\lambda_W,\lambda_P>0$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),nosep]
\item The collapsed point $(a_k,p_k)=(0,0)$ is locally stable for every $k$.
\item Direction $k$ has a non-collapsed fixed point if and only if $c_k^2\ge4\lambda_W\sigma_k$. For $c_k^2>4\lambda_W\sigma_k$ there are two, at $p=p_\pm^k$ (of the sign of $c_k$) with $a^{2}=\lambda_Pp/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$; the one at $p_-^k$ is a saddle and the one at $p_+^k$ is stable. At equality they merge into a saddle-node, which is not robustly attracting. Hence the final rank is at most $\#\{k:c_k^2\ge4\lambda_W\sigma_k\}$.
\item If $\lambda_W>0$ but $\lambda_P=0$, no direction has a non-collapsed fixed point: $\dot p_k=0$ with $a_k\neq0$ forces the least-squares gain $p_k=c_k/\sigma_k$, where $g_k=-\lambda_W<0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
Part (b) is the static threshold of \citet{tian2021understanding}, \citet{wang2021directset}, \citet{ponce2025dual} and \citet{arbel2026peira}, which we do not claim; by (c) a survivor needs a predictor shrunk below the least-squares gain.

\begin{corollary}[Exact scalar race for $\alpha=1$]
\label{cor:alpha1}
Under \cref{prop:reduction} with the copy target, $\alpha=1$ and $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$, the identity \eqref{eq:balance} restricted to direction $k$ gives $p_k^2-a_k^2=e^{-2\lambda t}\big(p_0^2-a_k(0)^2\big)$, so
\begin{equation}
\dot a_k=g_k(p_k)\,a_k,\qquad p_k=\sqrt{a_k^2+e^{-2\lambda t}\big(p_0^2-a_k(0)^2\big)} ,
\label{eq:alpha1}
\end{equation}
a closed scalar equation, exact for any imbalance (the per-mode integral of \citealp{tian2021understanding}, eq.~14).
In the balanced case $p_0=a_k(0)$ it is autonomous, $\dot a=(|a|c_k-a^2\sigma_k-\lambda)\,a$, and direction $k$ survives if and only if $|a_k(0)|>p_-^k\approx\lambda/c_k$.
\end{corollary}
The balanced case is the basin of \citet{tian2021understanding}. At a standard initialisation, however, $a_k(0)\ll p_0=O(1)$, and the gain decays at the rate of weight decay until the amplitude has grown to its size; which comes first is the race.

\subsection{The race in the unbalanced regime}
\label{sec:race}


\Cref{prop:fixed} says which end states exist. Which one training reaches depends on the side of the separatrix through the saddle at $p_-^k$ on which it starts, and for $a_k(0)\ll\min(1,p_0)$ the race locates that side.
As long as $a_k^2(c_k-p\sigma_k)\ll\lambda_Pp$, the data term in $\dot p_k$ is negligible and the gain decays freely, $p(t)=p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}$.
For $\alpha=1$ this is the small-amplitude limit of \cref{cor:alpha1}; for $\alpha\neq1$, an approximation.
The amplitude then integrates the net rate along the decaying gain (\cref{fig:drive-decay})
\begin{equation}
\log\frac{a_k(t)}{a_k(0)}\approx F_k(t)=\int_0^tg_k\big(p(s)\big)\,ds=\frac{c_k\big(p_0-p\big)-\tfrac{\sigma_k}{2}\big(p_0^2-p^2\big)}{\alpha\lambda_P}-\lambda_Wt .
\label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
$F_k$ grows while the gain is inside the window, so it peaks when the gain leaves it at $p_-^k$ (or at $t=0$); if $p_0>p_+^k$ it also counts the initial shrinking phase above the window.

\paragraph{Capture.}
Direction $k$ takes over its gain once its data drive on $p_k$ balances weight decay, that is once it crosses the nullcline $\dot p_k=0$ of \eqref{eq:growth}, $a^2=\lambda_Pp/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$.
Above it the gain is pulled up towards $p_+^k$ and the direction converges to the stable fixed point of \cref{prop:fixed}(b); capture helps only while the direction still grows ($p\ge p_-^k$).

\begin{definition}[Race criterion; an approximation]
\label{def:race}
Direction $k$ is predicted to survive if and only if
\begin{equation}
R_k=\max_{t\le T:\;p(t)\ge p_-^k}\Big[\log a_k(0)+F_k(t)-\tfrac12\log\frac{\lambda_P\,p(t)}{c_k-p(t)\,\sigma_k}\Big]\;\ge\;0 .
\label{eq:race}
\end{equation}
where $T$ is the training horizon.
The predicted rank is $\hat r=\#\{k:R_k\ge0\}$.
For $\lambda_P=0$ the gain does not drift, and direction $k$ is predicted to survive if it grows to the counting threshold $\theta$ (set in advance, not fitted) by $T$ (\cref{app:proofs}).
\end{definition}
The criterion depends only on $(c_k,\sigma_k)$ and $(p_0,a_k(0),\alpha,\lambda_W,\lambda_P,T)$; we write $a_0$ when all directions start at the same amplitude.
Without weight decay survival reduces to the sign of the growth rate at initialisation, $g_k(p_0)>0$; we call $\#\{k:g_k(p_0)>0\}$ the \emph{initial window} count, which in our model equals the stability count of \citet{demelocosta2026drive} with its encoder weight-decay floor (\cref{app:related}).
With weight decay the initial window is neither necessary nor sufficient: a gain above the window can decay into it, and a direction inside it still loses if its gain falls below $p_-^k$ before capture (\cref{fig:drive-decay}).

\paragraph{Critical weight decay.}
Evaluating the capture term at $p_-^k\approx\lambda_W/c_k$ and expanding for small weight decay, with $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$ and $\alpha=1$, direction $k$ survives when
\begin{equation}
h_k(p_0)\equiv c_kp_0-\tfrac12\sigma_kp_0^2\;\gtrsim\;\lambda\log\frac{p_0}{a_0},\qquad\text{so}\qquad \lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx\frac{h_k(p_0)}{\log(p_0/a_0)} .
\label{eq:lamrace}
\end{equation}
The log-growth the decaying gain allows before it leaves the window, $h_k(p_0)/\lambda$ to leading order, must exceed $\log(p_0/a_0)$; the threshold is thus far below the static $c_k^2/(4\sigma_k)$ and depends on the initial scale (for $c_k=\sigma_k=p_0=1$, $a_0=10^{-4}$: $0.05$ against $0.25$).
For $\alpha\ne1$ the logarithmic corrections of the expansion are comparable to $\log(1/a_0)$ (\cref{app:proofs}), so we use \eqref{eq:race}.

\paragraph{Design choices.}\label{sec:heuristics}
Each design choice enters the race through the data $(c_k,\sigma_k)$, the start $(p_0,a_0)$ or the clock $1/(\alpha\lambda_P)$: weight decay narrows the window and sets the clock, predictor weight decay is necessary for any survivor (\cref{prop:fixed}(c)), and a faster or deeper predictor shortens the clock (\cref{app:roles}, \cref{tab:heuristics}).
Without the stop-gradient every direction decays~\citep[Theorem~2]{tian2021understanding}, as \citet{halvagal2023implicit} also show.
